# net.list -- the Minix-derived TCP/IP stack.
#
# THE DAEMONS, THE CONFIGURATION AND THE NETWORK COMMANDS ONLY.  The programs
# that EXERCISE this stack -- echoclient/echoserver, the udp* pair, devtcp,
# psipping, netdbtest and the rest -- live in testing.list, which a release dist
# does not include.  A new entry here is a tool somebody runs; a new probe goes
# there.
#
# Three processes that rendezvous through named pipes: /etc/inet holds the protocol
# core and talks to a link layer (slip or ppp) over the psip pseudo-device, and
# clients reach it through the well-known FIFO /dev/inet plus a per-socket FIFO
# pair they mknod themselves (net/include/inet_ipc.h).
#
# /dev/inet is a named PIPE, which no /dev table can express -- mkimage writes
# block and character nodes only -- so it is created at boot with
# `mknod /dev/inet p'.  Named pipes were verified working on the target first.
#
# NEVER PUT A COMMENT IN /etc/rc OR /etc/rc.net.  This shell is V7-era and has no
# `#' comments, so a comment line is parsed as a command -- and worse, a
# backquote or apostrophe inside it is taken as quoting and SWALLOWS the
# following lines.  A commented rc silently lost the mknod, and rc has no console
# (init runs it on /dev/null) so nothing said why.  Explanations belong here.
#
# /etc/rc.net brings the stack up at boot: the rendezvous pipe, the daemon, the
# interface address, and slip on the serial line if that line is a device.  It
# ships from THIS list, and /etc/rc runs it only `if test -r' -- so a dist built
# without networking has no rc.net and its boot is unchanged.
#
# rc runs it in the BACKGROUND, and that is not cosmetic.  ifconfig's first act
# is open("/dev/inet", O_WRONLY), which blocks until /etc/inet opens the same pipe
# for reading: a real rendezvous, and the reason rc.net needs no `sleep' between
# starting the daemon and configuring the interface.  But it is also a wait with
# no timeout, so a daemon that dies during startup would hang the boot forever
# and the machine would never reach multi-user.  In the background it costs
# nothing but the network.
#
# The address lives at the top of rc.net as a shell variable, which is where a
# site edits it.  There is no DHCP and no hostname database in 3.x.  NTPSERVER
# is edited in the same place and defaults to 10.0.0.1, the SLIP peer -- the only
# host this machine can reach at all, so also the only one that can tell it what
# time it is.
#
# The sntp call sits AFTER the slip block, not next to ifconfig: the clock is set
# over the network, so the link has to exist first.  It is bounded on purpose
# (-t 5 -r 2, so ten seconds at worst) because a server that does not answer must
# cost a boot ten seconds and not a hang -- and because slip may still be coming
# up when it runs, which the second try covers.  It is not backgrounded: it exits
# either way, and anything after it in rc.net that timestamps a file wants the
# clock already right.
#
# EVERY GUARD IN rc.net IS `test -r'.  This test(1) (cmd/test/test.y) has
# exactly eight file operators -- -r -w -f -d -s -t -z -n -- and NOTHING ELSE.
# There is no -c and no -x.  An unknown operator is not a false answer, it is a
# parse error: test prints "Test expression syntax error" and exits non-zero, so
# the if takes the ELSE branch and the guarded daemon never starts, while
# everything before it comes up fine.  That is a working stack that answers no
# packets, or a shipped telnetd nobody can reach, and neither says why.
#
# A `test -c' or `test -x' guard therefore silently skips the daemon it
# guards.  -r is an access(2) call and is the operator rc itself already
# relies on to find rc.net; -f (exists and is not a directory) is the only
# other safe choice.
#
# rc.net's output goes to /tmp/rc.net.log.  init runs rc on /dev/null, so
# anything the daemons say at boot -- inet's `ready', ifconfig's error, slip
# refusing the line -- would otherwise be discarded, and a network that failed to
# come up would leave no evidence of why.  The redirection is on rc's side
# rather than an `exec' inside rc.net so it uses nothing beyond plain V7
# redirection.
#
# Built by hostbuild/build-net.sh (`make net'), which is NOT part of the
# userland sweep: the stack has its own Makefile and include tree.
#
# /etc/inet.conf is read by inet_config.c read_conf() before anything else; the
# daemon exits if it is missing.  One psip interface, marked `default' because
# read_conf() refuses to run without a default network.  The psip branch of the
# parser takes no address -- an interface's IP address is a separate
# NWIOSIPCONF ioctl, so a configuration utility is still owed.
#
# /dev/ip (in devices/hd.devices, major 10) must ALSO pre-exist: read_conf()
# stat()s it purely to learn which major to reuse, and fatal()s if it is absent.
# /etc/inet then mknod()s /dev/{ip,psip,tcp,udp}0 itself with that same major and
# hard-links the unnumbered names to them.  Major 10 has NO driver in drvl[] on
# purpose -- in this port every real transfer goes over the FIFO channel, so the
# nodes are inode bookkeeping only, and umknod() does not validate the major.
# PACKAGE (os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT).  A list with a `package' line is an
# addressable unit of a release channel; one without is not, and that is how
# testing.list stays out of one.
package  net
role     optional
# KINDS -- the packages this component publishes besides its runtime half.
# `bin' is the list below and is not written down; `src' is the complete
# corresponding source for every executable it installs, and `man' is the pages
# for those programs.  os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT argues both, and neither can be
# cut from a guess: -src refuses without the producer's source map, -man refuses
# when not one of these programs has a page.
kinds    src man dev

# THE LIBRARY THIS COMPONENT BUILDS.  libsocket.a is net's -- os/net/Makefile
# builds it out of libsocket.c, the channel layer, netdb and the resolver -- and
# its twenty-six Lexicon articles (accept(), bind(), socket(), select(), the
# net/proto/serv database calls, and the libsocket overview itself) are the
# manual for something this component ships.  They go in net-man for the same
# reason the pages for its programs do; the manual says which pages they are,
# because each one's class line names the library it documents.
libraries libsocket

# THE COMPILE-TIME INTERFACE -- net-dev, what somebody needs to build a program
# that talks to this stack, as opposed to net-src, which is what they need to
# rebuild the stack itself.
#
# inet_ipc.h is the interface in the strictest sense: it declares the request
# and reply framing of the well-known FIFO /dev/inet and the per-socket FIFO
# pair, which is the protocol between a client and /etc/inet, and a program
# that speaks it needs nothing else of net's.  inet_chan.h declares the channel
# library that speaks it for you -- ichan_open(), ichan_ioctl_rw() -- which is
# what netstat and every libsocket call go through.
#
# netdb.h, arpa/inet.h and net/ are the Berkeley and Minix declarations the
# socket calls take and return: struct servent and struct hostent, the
# address-conversion functions, and net/gen/, whose structures are the payload
# of the ioctls net/ioctl.h names.  A client compiles against these and against
# the TOOLCHAIN's <sys/socket.h>, <sys/select.h> and <netinet/in.h>, which
# c900-include publishes and this package does not duplicate.
#
# net/include/minix/ and netdb_priv.h are NOT here.  They are the daemon's own
# internals -- kernel message types, the resolver's private state -- and a
# header a client must not include is not part of an interface.
dev	/usr/include/inet_ipc.h	net/include/inet_ipc.h
dev	/usr/include/inet_chan.h	net/include/inet_chan.h
dev	/usr/include/netdb.h	net/include/netdb.h
dev	/usr/include/arpa/inet.h	net/include/arpa/inet.h
dev	/usr/include/net	net/include/net
# The library itself, which is the other half: the headers declare the calls and
# this is what resolves them.  It installs where -lsocket looks.
dev	/usr/lib/libsocket.a	net/libsocket.a

f	/etc/inet.conf	644 0 1
# The host and service databases.  gethostbyname() reads /etc/hosts BEFORE it
# asks the network, so these files answer with no packet, and they have to: the
# nameserver is the SLIP peer, so a machine that needed the DNS to find its peer
# could never ask.  libsocket carries a built-in copy of the well-known services
# so a machine missing that file can still reach them.
f	/etc/hosts	644 0 1
f	/etc/services	644 0 1
# The resolver's configuration -- and the switch that turns the resolver on.
# net/resolv/ is ported and linked into libsocket, and host(1), telnet and ftp
# all carry it, but netdb.c asks the network only when this file EXISTS
# (have_resolv() is an access(2) call on the path).  Without it a name that
# /etc/hosts does not know is HOST_NOT_FOUND at once; with it, that name costs a
# query and, if nothing answers, four rounds of a doubling timeout -- about a
# minute, which reads as a hang.  So it ships from THIS list and not from
# base.list: a dist built without networking should not pay for a nameserver it
# cannot reach.  It names 10.0.0.1, the far end of the SLIP link, which is the
# only host this machine can reach at all.
f	/etc/resolv.conf	644 0 1
# This machine's own name.  There is no uname(2) and no hostname in the kernel,
# so gethostname() reads this file; without it every program that asks its own
# name is told "localhost" and looks itself up as 127.0.0.1.  hunt's client
# does exactly that to decide which network to search for a game driver on, so
# a missing file here is not cosmetic -- it makes the machine unable to find a
# server running on itself.  It holds the FULL name, c900.localnet, which is the
# canonical name of 10.0.0.2 in /etc/hosts: gethostname() must resolve to this
# machine's interface address, and the domain part is also what res_init() falls
# back to for the search list.  Keep in step with IFADDR in rc.net, /etc/hosts
# and the nameserver line in /etc/resolv.conf.
f	/etc/hostname	644 0 1
f	/etc/rc.net	755 0 1
# /etc/inet is the TCP/IP STACK, and the name says so.  It shipped as
# /etc/inetd for the whole port, which named it after a program it is not: BSD's
# inetd(8) is a super-server that listens on a table of ports and execs a
# program per connection, and this reads no such table and execs nothing.  It is
# the Minix inet server (net/inet/, `replaces Minix inet.c'), and every
# connection on this machine lives inside it.  The switchboard now ships under
# the name it earned, three lines down.
f	/etc/inet	755 0 1	src=net/inet/inet
# /etc/inetd -- the switchboard, and now the only thing by that name.  It reads
# /etc/inetd.conf, listens on each service's port, and runs a program per
# connection.  An ordinary CLIENT of /etc/inet: it links libsocket like telnet
# does and shares no code with the stack.
#
# It cannot hand a program the socket the way BSD's inetd does.  A connection
# here is two FIFOs plus framing state inside the process holding them, and
# exec(2) discards that state -- so the service is run on a PIPE with inetd
# relaying between pipe and socket, which costs one extra process per connection
# and buys a service that needs to know nothing about this stack.  The internal
# services (echo, daytime, ...) pay neither: the forked child answers on the
# socket itself.
#
# FOUR OF THIS MACHINE'S DAEMONS ARE SERVED FROM HERE.  telnetd, fingerd, remshd
# and smtpd each have an inetd mode -- read the connection on standard input,
# answer on standard output, one connection, exit -- and each finds the peer's
# and this machine's addresses in INETD_REMADDR/INETD_REMPORT/INETD_LOCADDR/
# INETD_LOCPORT, because a service on a pipe cannot ask the descriptor who is at
# the other end.  One binary does both jobs; the variables' presence is what
# selects the mode, and rc.net starts none of the four.
# smtpd's line ships enabled even though the program comes from
# lists/mail-light.list: inetd offers no service whose program is not installed,
# so the line is conditional on the mail list with nothing commented out and
# nothing to edit (see inetd.conf).
#
# AND ONE UDP SERVICE, WHICH inetd ANSWERS ITSELF: ntalk (518/udp), the daemon
# that used to be /etc/talkd.  A datagram service is BSD's `wait' class -- the
# program is given the listening socket rather than a connection -- and a
# descriptor cannot be handed to an exec'd program here at all, so it is an
# INTERNAL service: inetd binds the port and forks a child WITHOUT exec, which is
# how the socket arrives with libsocket's framing state intact.  That child holds
# the invitation table and retires when it holds nothing (net/talkserv.c), so
# between calls the machine spends no process on it and no separate 41 KB image.
# huntd is still the one thing not servable from here, and it needs no help: the
# client starts it on demand.
#
# rc.net starts it only when BOTH the program and the conf file are readable;
# deleting /etc/inetd.conf is how a site turns the switchboard off without
# rebuilding an image.
f	/etc/inetd	755 0 1	src=net/inetd
# The service table.  Six lines ship enabled -- the internal echo, daytime and
# ntalk, and telnet, finger and shell -- and every enabled line costs a stack
# connection for as long as the machine is up, which is why the file's own
# comments carry the arithmetic rather than leaving it to be rediscovered.
f	/etc/inetd.conf	644 0 1
f	/etc/slip	755 0 1	src=net/slip
f	/etc/ifconfig	755 0 1	src=net/ifconfig
f	/etc/ppp	755 0 1	src=net/ppp
# sntp(8) -- the machine's only automatic clock source.  read_cmos() returns 0
# (sys/z8001/src/mdstub.c) and the M58321 RTC has no driver, so the C900 boots at
# the epoch EVERY time: without this, 1970 is the date on every file it writes,
# make(1) cannot tell what is out of date, and find -newer answers nothing.  A
# one-shot ntpdate(8)-shaped client, not a daemon -- the kernel's whole
# time-setting interface is stime(2), which steps; there is no adjtime and no
# settimeofday, so clock DISCIPLINE is not available to write.  Run again from
# cron for a machine that stays right.
#
# It ships from net.list rather than testing.list because it is a tool somebody
# runs, and it is in /etc rather than /bin for the same reason ifconfig is:
# stime(2) is root-only, so a non-root invocation can only ever print the answer
# (which -q does, and which needs no privilege at all).
f	/etc/sntp	755 0 1	src=net/sntp
# The routing table: pr_routes prints it, add_route/del_route change it.  Minix
# 2.0.4's tools, unchanged in what they do.
#
# Reading the table is one _IORW ioctl -- an entry number out, that entry back --
# which the channel library could not express until ichan_ioctl_rw(); before
# that, nothing on this machine could SEE a route, only add one.
#
# add_route and del_route are the same program under two names (it decides from
# argv[0]), staged twice because a list has no way to say `hard link'.
f	/etc/pr_routes	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/pr_routes
# netstat(1) -- what this machine can say about its own network state.  WRITTEN
# here, not ported: COHERENT 4.x had one (its Lexicon page survives, and its
# option letters are the ones this takes), but 4.x read protocol control blocks
# out of the KERNEL through /dev/kmem, and this stack's connection table is
# inside a user process.  Nothing of that program's body could survive; only its
# interface was worth keeping.
#
# It reports what the daemon answers and nothing else: the interfaces
# /etc/inet.conf declares with the address NWIOGIPCONF gives back (-i), BOTH
# routing tables (-r, the outgoing one being pr_routes' data), and the open
# channels -- one per socket, filtered by kill(pid,0) so an abandoned FIFO pair
# is counted as abandoned rather than listed as a socket (-a).  There is no -m
# and no connection endpoint list, because the buffer pool and the connection
# table are private to the daemon; -s exists because NWIOGETHSTAT is real, and
# says so plainly when no ethernet interface is configured rather than printing
# a screen of zeroes.  It starts working the day the LANCE driver lands.
#
# In /bin and not /etc: it needs no privilege, and the Lexicon page puts the
# plain form in a user's path.
#
# CAUTION, and inherited: NWIOGIPCONF does not answer `unconfigured'.  ip_ioctl()
# SUSPENDS the request until the interface has an address, so `netstat' run
# before /etc/ifconfig hangs with no output instead of printing an empty table.
# ifconfig has the same property; -a is the one form that needs no daemon at all.
f	/bin/netstat	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/netstat
f	/etc/add_route	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/add_route
f	/etc/del_route	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/add_route
# telnet(1) -- Minix 2.0.4's ttn.c through libsocket's /dev/tcp shim.  It puts
# the local line in raw mode with TCGETA/TCSETA, so it wants a kernel built
# KTTY=termio; on the sgtty kernel the ioctls fail and the line stays cooked.
# Escape is ~. (at the start of a line).
f	/bin/telnet	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/telnet
# ftp(1) -- Michael Temari's client from the same Minix drop, same shim.  Its
# `lmkdir'/`lrmdir' go through libc's mkdir()/rmdir(), which run /bin/mkdir and
# /bin/rmdir: this kernel has no mkdir(2), and mknod(IFDIR) is root only.
f	/bin/ftp	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/ftp
# host(1) is the DNS query tool -- Minix 2 has no nslookup.  Since
# /etc/resolv.conf now ships, a name that /etc/hosts does not know goes to the
# nameserver at 10.0.0.1; delete that file and the same lookup fails at once
# instead of spending the full retry schedule.  This is the only program here
# whose whole job is the network half, so it is what says whether DNS works --
# telnet and ftp would answer from /etc/hosts and look identical either way.
f	/bin/host	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/host
# ping(1) -- the one client whose whole job is IP itself.  It reaches the stack
# through libsocket's /dev/ip shim rather than /dev/tcp, so it is also what says
# the raw-IP half of that shim works; /bin/psipping tests the same ICMP path
# from the LINK side, by injecting a packet the stack must answer, and needs no
# peer.  This needs one: an address that answers, which over the SLIP line means
# the machine at the far end.
f	/bin/ping	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/ping
f	/bin/rlogin	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/rlogin
f	/bin/finger	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/finger
# fingerd answers what finger(1) asks.  Until now this machine shipped the
# client alone, so it could question other hosts and none could question it.
# The protocol body is Minix's in.fingerd.c.  It runs /bin/finger on a pipe and
# needs no pty.
#
# IT IS AN /etc/inetd.conf SERVICE NOW, not an rc.net daemon, and so are telnetd
# and remshd: each reads the connection on its standard input, answers on its
# standard output and exits, and finds the peer's address in the environment
# because a program on a pipe cannot ask the descriptor.  The same binary still
# does the standalone job when the switchboard's variables are absent, which is
# how a site that wants one of them resident runs it by hand.
#
# Both listeners cost the same, whoever holds them: a channel costs the DAEMON
# two descriptors (request and reply FIFO) out of NUFILE = 20, of which its own
# stdio and the rendezvous pipe already take four, so the machine has eight
# channels in all.  Under the switchboard these three services spend three of
# them waiting, one per port, and the rest stay free for a connection made from
# this machine to itself.
f	/etc/fingerd	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/fingerd
# The remote shell, client and daemon.  They are NOT called rsh/rshd: /usr/bin/rsh
# on this system is COHERENT's restricted shell and has been since V7, so the
# BSD program takes the name HP-UX gave it over the same collision.
#
# remshd serves the single-connection form of the protocol only: the optional
# second connection for standard error, and with it signal forwarding, is not
# implemented, so a command's stderr comes back merged into its stdout.  remsh
# asks for that form; a stock BSD rsh asking for the back channel is refused
# with a message saying so.
f	/bin/remsh	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/remsh
f	/etc/remshd	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/remshd
# NO .rhosts IS SHIPPED, for root or for anybody, so out of the box remshd binds
# the port and refuses every caller: iruserok() finds no ~luser/.rhosts and no
# /etc/hosts.equiv, and a session is refused with "Permission denied."  A site
# turns the daemon on by writing one.
#
# THAT FILE CANNOT BE ROOT'S.  remshd refuses uid 0 outright (cmd/remshd), for
# the reason login(1) refuses it off the console: root's /etc/passwd field is
# empty so the machine's own keyboard is never locked out of itself, and a
# daemon that authenticates on an ADDRESS and asks for no password would turn
# that into a root shell for whoever the address database names.  ~root/.rhosts
# is therefore read by nothing.
#
# Mode 644 is not cosmetic on the one a site writes: iruserok() ignores a .rhosts
# that group or other can write, because a file the world can append to grants
# the account to the world.  The file must be owned by the account it lets into,
# or by root.
# talk needs an ntalk service at BOTH ends: the CALLER's daemon takes its
# LEAVE_INVITE and the CALLEE's takes the ANNOUNCE and writes on the terminal, so
# the client could not complete a call in either direction until one existed.  It
# is /etc/inetd's now, and no longer a daemon of its own.
f	/bin/talk	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/talk
# THERE IS NO /etc/talkd.  The daemon /bin/talk needs at both ends is the ntalk
# line in /etc/inetd.conf, answered inside the switchboard (net/talkserv.c): the
# invitation table lives in a child inetd forks without exec, and nothing is
# resident between calls.  Root is still required, and not because 518 is
# privileged -- it is not, here -- but because announcing a call means writing on
# another user's terminal.
# telnetd is an /etc/inetd.conf service: one login per connection, read on
# standard input and answered on standard output.  Minix's model, where tcpd
# listens and hands the connection to an exec'd program, still cannot work here
# -- with the user-space /dev/tcp shim a connection is two FIFO fds plus the
# channel's framing state, none of which survives exec -- so the switchboard
# relays over a pipe instead, which is what an inetd mode had to be written
# against.  Needs a KTTY=termio kernel for the pty driver.
#
# The standalone pre-forking server is still in the same binary and rc.net no
# longer starts it: `/etc/telnetd -m 3 &' by hand is what a site that wants
# resident listeners runs, with the telnet line commented out of inetd.conf.
# Either way a session takes a pty when the connection arrives and not before;
# the kernel has NUPTY=8 channels (sys/drv/pty.c), shared with screen(1) and
# with local use, so that -- or inetd's child limit -- is the real bound on how
# many logins at once.
f	/etc/telnetd	755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/telnetd
# screen(1) -- GNU screen 3.2 in MWC's own COHERENT port.
# It needs the pty driver, so a KTTY=termio kernel; with NUPTY=8 channels shared
# with telnetd it is a handful of windows, not ten.  setuid+setgid root
# because a pty master is mode 600
# root and screen has to open one; its lock/password, utmp and remote-detach code
# is compiled OUT, which is what a setuid program here should carry least of.
# /tmp/screens must exist and be sticky, or the first run cannot make its socket.
f	/usr/bin/screen	6511 0 0	src=hostbuild/build/bin/screen
f	/etc/screenrc	644 0 0	src=net/screen/etcscreenrc
d	/tmp/screens	1777 0 0
